WebAssembly target support added to DMD

DMD now recognizes WebAssembly as a compilation target via the `-mwasm32` switch.
This can be used to run D code in web browsers, or in local sandbox environments such as `wasmtime`.
See also: https://github.com/jcbhmr/awesome-webassembly-runtimes

With wasm-ld and wasmtime installed, and added to PATH (or set in the `WASMTIME` environment variable), try:

```D
import core.stdc.stdio;

void main()
{
    printf("Hello WebAssembly!");
}
```

$(CONSOLE
$ dmd -mwasm32 hello.d
$ wasmtime run hello.wasm
)

Or:

$(CONSOLE
$ dmd -mwasm32 -run hello.d
)

WebAssembly is the architecture. The `-os` switch selects the runtime
environment: which C library, startup code and linker to use, and which version
identifiers are predefined. These match the target triples of LDC and clang:

$(TABLE
    $(THEAD `-os`, triple, links with, output)
    $(TROW `wasi` or `wasip1`, `wasm32-unknown-wasip1`, `wasm-ld` and wasi-libc (default for `-mwasm32`), `.wasm`)
    $(TROW `wasip2`, `wasm32-unknown-wasip2`, not supported yet, )
    $(TROW `emscripten`, `wasm32-unknown-emscripten`, `emcc`, `.js` + `.wasm`)
)

By default, WASI programs are linked by invoking `wasm-ld` directly with the
wasi-libc `libc.a` and C headers that the Phobos build places next to
`libphobos2-wasm.a`. Set the `WASM_CC` environment
variable to link through a C compiler driver instead (`WASM_CC=wasm32-wasip1-clang`), or set `WASM_LD` to override
the `wasm-ld` executable.

`-os=emscripten` links with Emscripten's `emcc` (override with `WASM_CC`),
which brings its own C library, JavaScript runtime and ports (SDL, OpenGL, a
virtual file system). The output is a `.js` loader next to the `.wasm` module,
and `-run` uses `node` (override with `NODE`). Emscripten needs its own druntime
and Phobos build: `make -C phobos WASM_OS=emscripten wasm`.

ImportC sources are preprocessed with `clang --target=wasm32-wasip1` against
the wasi-libc headers, or with `$WASM_CC -E` / `emcc -E`.

Version identifiers describing the architecture:
$(UL
    $(LI `WebAssembly`)
    $(LI `LittleEndian`)
    $(LI `D_SIMD`)
)

Version identifiers describing the platform:
$(UL
    $(LI `WASI`, also for Emscripten, like LDC)
    $(LI `WASIp1` or `WASIp2`)
    $(LI `Emscripten`, for `-os=emscripten`)
    $(LI `Posix`, since `wasi-libc` and Emscripten implement a subset of Posix)
    $(LI `CRuntime_WASI`, or `CRuntime_Musl` for Emscripten)
    $(LI `CppRuntime_LLVM`, `CppRuntime_Clang`)
)

The .wasm should be binary compatible with ldc2.

To specify the module of exported symbols, use the `wasmImportModule` attribute:

```D
import core.attribute : wasmImportModule;
@wasmImportModule("wasi_snapshot_preview1") extern (C) void proc_exit(int code);

// LDC way:
import ldc.attributes;
@llvmAttr("wasm-import-module", "wasi_snapshot_preview1")
extern (C) void proc_exit(int code);
```

To export a function to the host under a chosen name, use the `wasmExportName`
attribute. The function is kept by the linker and exported without
`--export-dynamic`, and the export name may differ from the symbol name:

```D
import core.attribute : wasmExportName;
@wasmExportName("windowStep") extern (C) void internalStep() { }

// LDC way:
import ldc.attributes;
@llvmAttr("wasm-export-name", "windowStep")
extern (C) void internalStep() { }
```

Phobos is linked by default, just like on the native targets. Building it
mirrors the native library: `make -C phobos wasm` produces `libphobos2-wasm.a`
(which, like `libphobos2.a`, also contains druntime) in
`phobos/generated/wasm/release/wasm32`, and `dmd.conf` points `-mwasm32` at it
(the `[Environmentwasm32]` section, or `[Environmentwasm32-emscripten]`).
As usual, `-defaultlib=` selects a different library, and an empty
`-defaultlib=` opts out of the runtime entirely.

```D
import std.stdio;

void main()
{
    writeln("hello from ", "wasm");
}
```

Limitations:
- No deprecated complex number types like `cfloat` and `ifloat`
- `real` is 64-bit, not 128-bit
